Comment (policies/elbv2_asset_disposal.rego:58):
Medium: `startswith(event_name, "Delete")` is too broad — it matches `DeleteListener` and `DeleteRule`, not just `DeleteLoadBalancer`. A failed/inactive load balancer whose only delete event is a listener or rule deletion is wrongly credited with disposal audit evidence, so the violation never fires.

This also contradicts the design intent recorded in git notes ("keeping load balancer disposal events separate from listener/rule change evidence"). The management-change policy already uses an explicit `event_name` allowlist; suggest matching the load-balancer disposal event specifically, e.g.:

```rego
delete_event_exists if {
    event := events[_]
    object.get(event, "event_name", "") == "DeleteLoadBalancer"
}
```
---
Comment (policies/elbv2_edge_endpoint_inventory.rego:68):
Low: both this rule and the missing-DNS rule above (line 62) emit the identical violation id `{"id": "endpoint_not_inventoried"}`. Because `violation` is a set, the two distinct conditions collapse into a single element — `count(violation)` can never exceed 1 even when both DNS and owner are missing, and a consumer cannot tell which condition fired.

The management-change policy uses distinct ids per condition; suggest the same here, e.g. `dns_not_inventoried` and `owner_not_identified`.
---
Comment (policies/elbv2_edge_endpoint_inventory.rego:64):
Low: silent gap on unknown scheme. The violation rules only fire when `scheme == "internet-facing"`, and `skip_reason` is only set for non-loadbalancer records or `scheme == "internal"`. A `loadbalancer` whose `scheme` is empty/missing/unexpected is therefore neither evaluated nor skipped — it silently passes with no evidence and no reason. Consider treating an unknown scheme as a skip-with-reason (or a violation) so it is not a silent hole in coverage.
